# GnuchanOS - the packages the live image carries.
#
# live-build installs this list into the chroot. It holds three kinds of
# package, and they are kept together because a live image is not a package
# set with optional parts: what is not here is not on the ISO.
#
#   1. the base of a live X session - init, a shell, the X libraries, fonts,
#      the input and video drivers under XLibre;
#   2. the tools the Gnuchan programs are BUILT with, because the hook
#      compiles them inside the image (the programs' own makefiles would ask
#      for these one at a time; naming them here is one apt run instead of
#      ten, and it is the same list either way);
#   3. small programs the session needs to be usable out of the box.
#
# XLibre itself is NOT here: it is not in Debian, so it is added by a hook
# from its own repository before this list is installed.

# --- a bootable live system ---
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
systemd
systemd-sysv
dbus

# --- the boot splash. plymouth is what draws the GnuchanOS theme the Plymouth
#     hook installs; without the daemon and its script module the hook's theme
#     has nothing to draw it and the boot falls back to text. The initramfs
#     tools are what put the theme inside the initramfs, where plymouth runs
#     from before the root filesystem is mounted. ---
plymouth
plymouth-themes
initramfs-tools

# --- GRUB, for the ISO's own boot menu to be themed. The theme installer
#     (dotfile/GRUB_THEME/settings_grub.py) REFUSES to render a theme on a
#     machine with no GRUB - it answers "there is no menu to theme" and stops -
#     so without grub-common the chroot never builds the GnuChanOS theme and the
#     binary hook (0080-boot-branding) has nothing to copy into the ISO's boot
#     menu. This is not GRUB as a boot loader (the ISO's is its own); it is the
#     theme files and the tools the installer looks for. ---
grub-common

# --- the X server's own parts (XLibre provides the server; these are what it
#     is built against and what its drivers need) ---
xkb-data
x11-xkb-utils
x11-utils
x11-xserver-utils
xauth
xinit
keyboard-configuration
# The touchpad: the library, the X driver that loads it, and the two tools the
# touchpad configuration is applied with.
#
#   libinput10                 the libinput shared library. Everything below
#                              links against it: the X driver, a Wayland
#                              compositor, and the Gnuchan programs that read
#                              input themselves.
#   xserver-xorg-input-libinput the X server's INPUT DRIVER. This is the piece
#                              that is easy to miss and fatal to miss: the
#                              touchpad configuration at
#                              /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/90-gnuchan-touchpad.conf
#                              names Driver "libinput", and with no driver by
#                              that name on the image the X server ignores the
#                              file and the pad falls back to its defaults -
#                              no tap to click, no two-finger scroll, no middle
#                              button. The libinput LIBRARY above is not the
#                              driver and does not stand in for it.
#   xinput                     the tool that pushes a touchpad setting into the
#                              session that is running. The X server reads its
#                              configuration once, at startup, so without this
#                              a setting written now takes effect only at the
#                              next login. Both the window manager (wm_input.c)
#                              and dotfile/TOUCHPAD_SETTINGS apply settings
#                              through it.
#   libinput-tools             the `libinput` command, for reading a device's
#                              capabilities when a pad does not behave.
libinput10
xserver-xorg-input-libinput
xinput
libinput-tools
udev
libgl1
libgl1-mesa-dri
libegl1
libgbm1
procps
pciutils
usbutils

# --- fonts ---
xfonts-base
fonts-dejavu-core
fonts-dejavu-extra
fonts-liberation2

# --- the tools the Gnuchan programs are built with ---
build-essential
pkg-config
python3
python3-setuptools
ca-certificates
curl
gnupg
git

# --- what the programs link against ---
libx11-dev
libxft-dev
libfreetype-dev
libxcursor-dev
libimlib2-dev
libxcomposite-dev
libxdamage-dev
libxrender-dev
libxrandr-dev
libxss-dev
libxext-dev
libxmu-dev
libxpm-dev
libpng-dev
libjpeg-dev
# GnuChanSS's optional D-Bus client. Without this header the screen saver
# builds WITHOUT the inhibit name, so a video (Firefox, Chromium, mpv, VLC)
# cannot tell it to hold off and the saver drops over a film once the idle
# clock passes — the exact fault the name exists to prevent. It is a build
# dependency of the live image and not only of a development machine.
libdbus-1-dev
# GnuChanDM (the display manager) authenticates through PAM, so it is built
# against libpam's header. It is listed here rather than left to the DM's own
# makefile, which would apt-install it mid-build: the image should carry what
# its programs are built from, and one apt run at image time is one fewer thing
# to go wrong inside the chroot.
libpam0g-dev
libpam-modules
libpam-runtime
# The X server wrapper that reads Xwrapper.config. It is what lets a normal
# user start X, which is how the live session comes up (see the session hook).
xserver-xorg-legacy
# dbus-x11 provides the session bus helpers a desktop session expects; the
# notification server and every program that talks to it want a session bus.
dbus-x11

# --- GnuChanBrowser is a Qt program ---
qt6-base-dev
qt6-base-dev-tools
libqt6widgets6
libqt6network6
libqt6webenginewidgets6
libqt6webenginecore6

# --- networking, so the live image is actually usable online ---
# NetworkManager is what gives `nmcli`, the little applet in the bar and the
# whole connect-to-wifi flow. It is not optional on a live image: a machine
# that boots with no way to join a network is a machine that can do nothing
# with the network it was downloaded onto. wpasupplicant is what NetworkManager
# drives to authenticate a WPA network; without it every secured network is
# refused with no visible reason.
network-manager
wpasupplicant
wireless-tools
rfkill
# systemd-resolved is the resolver GnuChanNetworkManager drives with resolvectl,
# and the image was going without it: `resolvectl` answered "command not found",
# so the manager's per-link DNS path had nothing to call and every lookup left
# the machine as plain port-53 traffic. That matters beyond tidiness — on a
# network that filters DNS BY NAME (a blocked site answers google.com but
# refuses discord.com from the same 8.8.8.8), plain DNS cannot be escaped by
# changing servers, and the only way out is encrypted DNS. systemd-resolved is
# what provides it: DNS-over-TLS, set with `resolvectl dnsovertls <link> yes`,
# which encrypts the query so a middlebox cannot read the name and refuse it.
# It is also what makes the manager's own resolvectl commands work at all.
systemd-resolved
# The kernel firmware the wireless cards need. The firmware is not optional and
# is easy to leave out by accident: a card with no firmware has NO interface at
# all, so `nmcli` shows nothing to connect to and the card looks like it is not
# supported. firmware-linux pulls the lot; the named ones are the common
# wireless chipsets and are listed so a build that trims the metapackage still
# carries them.
firmware-linux
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-atheros
firmware-realtek
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-ralink
firmware-misc-nonfree

# The kernel firmware the GRAPHICS cards need. This block exists for the same
# reason as the wireless one above and was the same omission: a GPU whose
# firmware is not on the image does not come up, and the machine falls back to
# software rendering (llvmpipe) or a black screen - which looks like "this
# distribution does not support my card". Each of the three vendors is named
# separately even though firmware-linux pulls most of them, so that a trimmed
# metapackage still carries them.
#
#   firmware-amd-graphics    every modern AMD card and Ryzen APU needs this to
#                            light up at all; without it amdgpu cannot bring
#                            the card out of reset.
#   firmware-nvidia-graphics  what nouveau loads on Pascal (GTX 10xx) and
#                            newer; without it those cards stay on the VESA
#                            framebuffer.
#   firmware-intel-graphics  GuC/HuC blobs for Skylake and newer Intel
#                            graphics; older Intel (965GM and the like) needs
#                            none.
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-nvidia-graphics
firmware-intel-graphics
# The GPU-side userspace. libgl1-mesa-dri (already listed) is the OpenGL
# driver for every vendor; these three are the Vulkan loader and the per-vendor
# Vulkan drivers, so an application that asks for Vulkan (a game, an emulator)
# finds it instead of falling back to OpenGL or failing.
libvulkan1
mesa-vulkan-drivers

# --- the torrent client and its bindings, from dotfile/Install_Software.py.
#     deluge is the client, and its daemon (deluged) and web UI ship inside it;
#     deluge-gtk is the windowed front end; python3-libtorrent is the library
#     the client runs on. They are named here so the live image carries what a
#     machine that ran Install_Software.py carries, and the services that the
#     script turns on (ssh, irqbalance, ntpsec) are enabled by the
#     0250-install-software hook. ---
deluge
deluge-gtk
python3-libtorrent

# --- the session's helpers: sound, a browser backend, the pointer theme ---
alsa-utils
xterm
x11-apps
nano
sudo
irqbalance
ntpsec
openssh-server

# --- locale and time ---
locales
tzdata
